This request is inspired by https://github.com/rstudio/renv/issues/307. Renv has a nice way to set authentication headers <https://rstudio.github.io/renv/articles/renv.html?q=renv.download.headers#authentication-with-custom-headers>

```R
options(renv.download.headers = function(url) {
  if (grepl("^https://my/repository", url))
    return(c(Authorization = Sys.getenv("AUTH_HEADER")))
})
```

Is there a way to do this in packrat?

@kevinushey - I'm trying to understand how I can set the R_PACKRAT_CACHE_DIR environment variable to be r version specific. 

In my experience the packrat cache is r major version specific and you will get errors saying that a package has been built by a version of R with different internals if you just use one for multiple r versions on a system (system being widows for me).

I've been setting it dynamically in ".~/.Rprofile" but this is then overridden by the projects .Rprofile when I open projects using the .Rproj file. Are there any workarounds?

The function name `with_extlib` suggests to me that I can execute an expression with the `extlib` library-path available to the expression. I understand that the function purpose is really "run something with an external-library *package* loaded temporarily", in which case I interpret it to really *mean* `with_extlib_package`. I'm not suggesting changing the name (likely too hard), but I would hope to be able to do something like:

```r
packrat::with_extlib(expr=packageVersion("testthat"))
```

This fails for two reasons, obviously. If I instead do

```r
packrat::with_extlib(character(0), packageVersion("testthat"))
```

I can bypass the first safeguard (`(is.character(packages)`), but unfortunately the presence of the external library is only given to the `library` call, not the expression itself.

This is a specific use-case, granted, though I can envision a more-extensive expression that relies on a package and perhaps implied dependencies. For instance, perhaps I need to access a specific function within the package but do not want to full attach it, e.g., `package::variable`, which results in the namespace being loaded but not attached to the search path.

The following code works (when I strong-arm things in `browser`).

```diff
     local({
         tryCatch({
             oldSearch <- search()
             libPaths <- .packrat_mutables$get("origLibPaths")
+            oldLibPaths <- .libPaths()
             if (!length(libPaths)) 
                 libPaths <- getDefaultLibPaths()
+            .libPaths(libPaths)
             for (package in packages) {
                 library(package, character.only = TRUE, lib.loc = libPaths, 
                   warn.conflicts = FALSE)
             }
             error <- try(res <- eval(call$expr, envir = envir), 
                 silent = TRUE)
             if (exists("res", envir = environment(), inherits = FALSE)) {
                 res
             }
             else {
                 stop(attr(error, "condition")$message, call. = FALSE)
             }
         }, finally = {
             newSearch <- search()
             for (path in setdiff(newSearch, oldSearch)) {
                 try(forceUnload(path))
             }
+            .libPaths(oldLibPaths)
         })
     })
```

The use of `lib.loc = libPaths` is not strictly required. It could be extended to be `.libPaths(c(libPaths, oldLibPaths))` if you feel the need, I don't have a strong feeling at this point.

I can submit a PR if this discussion is fruitful, what do you think of this type of change?

